Subject: [PATCH] clk: rockchip: px30: save/restore CRU mode and armclk
 divider across suspend/resume

Not ported from upstream - this is a new workaround, modeled directly
on the equivalent, already-upstream mechanism in
drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-rk3288.c (rk3288_clk_suspend/rk3288_clk_resume),
which exists to fix the same class of bug on a different (older)
Rockchip SoC in this same family.

rk3288's suspend-side comment explains the underlying issue plainly:

    Some CRU registers will be reset in maskrom when the system
    wakes up from fastboot. So save them before suspend, restore
    them after resume.

    Switch PLLs other than DPLL (for SDRAM) to slow mode to avoid
    crashes on resume. The Mask ROM on the system will put APLL,
    CPLL, and GPLL into slow mode at resume time anyway (which is
    why we restore them), but we might not even make it to the Mask
    ROM if this isn't done at suspend time.

    NOTE: only APLL truly matters here, but we'll do them all.

clk-px30.c has never had any equivalent of this at all - it
`#include <linux/syscore_ops.h>` but never calls
register_syscore_ops(), so that include has been dead code since the
driver was written. No CRU register is saved before suspend or
restored after resume on this SoC.

This is suspected to be the root cause of a real, reproducible bug on
the GKD Pixel2 (RK3326S/PX30S): the system crashes (unrelated-process
memory corruption, a PUD-level translation fault in unmap_page_range)
specifically when a CPU frequency transition happens after a
suspend-to-RAM/resume cycle - not tied to a specific frequency, not
tied to elapsed time since resume, and not reproduced at all if the
CPU is left pinned at a single fixed frequency (performance or
powersave) for extended periods post-resume. That signature - fine
until something reprograms the CPU clock, then wrong - matches a
stale/reset CRU register being read by clk-cpu.c's safe-parent-switch
sequence (rockchip_cpuclk_pre_rate_change/post_rate_change), which
assumes the CPU core divider register (PX30_CLKSEL_CON(0), the same
register rockchip_cpuclk_recalc_rate() reads back) still holds
whatever Linux last configured it to, rather than whatever PX30S's
own resume path may have reset it to.

Trimmed relative to rk3288's version: only PX30_MODE_CON (PLL
slow/normal mode select, the direct architectural equivalent of what
rk3288 saves) and PX30_CLKSEL_CON(0) (the armclk/CPU-core divider
register - the specific register this board's bug points at) are
saved/restored. rk3288's suspend-side also force-switches PLLs to
slow mode before suspend and holds a specific clock gate open for a
PMU_CLR_DMA prerequisite particular to that SoC's own deep-sleep
sequencing; neither is ported here since PX30S's deep suspend is
governed by a completely different mechanism (BL31 SIP calls via
rockchip-pm-config, see 031-px30s-suspend-wakeup-config.patch) and
there is no evidence either is needed on this SoC - only the
save-before/restore-after half is applicable.

Confirmed fixing the post-resume cpufreq-transition crash on real
hardware (kernel 6.12.79).

Rebased for the kernel 6.12 -> 7.1.2 migration: upstream reworked the
generic syscore_ops mechanism (drivers/base/syscore.c /
include/linux/syscore_ops.h) - callbacks now take a `void *data`
argument instead of `void`, registration no longer embeds a list_head
directly in `struct syscore_ops` but instead wraps it in a separate
`struct syscore { struct list_head node; const struct syscore_ops
*ops; void *data; }`, and `register_syscore_ops()` was replaced by
`register_syscore(struct syscore *)`. clk-rk3288.c in this same
kernel tree was already updated upstream to the new API
(rk3288_clk_suspend/rk3288_clk_resume taking `void *data`, a static
`struct syscore rk3288_clk_syscore = { .ops = &rk3288_clk_syscore_ops
}`, and `register_syscore(&rk3288_clk_syscore)`) - this rebase follows
that exact pattern, no logic changes.

---
--- a/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-px30.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-px30.c
@@ -999,6 +999,55 @@
 	"pclk_usb_grf",
 };
 
+static void __iomem *px30_cru_base;
+
+/*
+ * PX30_MODE_CON (PLL slow/normal mode select) and PX30_CLKSEL_CON(0)
+ * (the armclk/CPU-core divider) are reset across suspend/resume on
+ * this SoC, the same way rk3288's equivalent CRU registers are reset
+ * by maskrom on that chip - see rk3288_clk_suspend()/rk3288_clk_resume()
+ * in clk-rk3288.c for the precedent this is modeled on. Save them
+ * before suspend, restore them after resume.
+ */
+static const int px30_saved_cru_reg_ids[] = {
+	PX30_MODE_CON,
+	PX30_CLKSEL_CON(0),
+};
+
+static u32 px30_saved_cru_regs[ARRAY_SIZE(px30_saved_cru_reg_ids)];
+
+static int px30_clk_suspend(void *data)
+{
+	int i, reg_id;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(px30_saved_cru_reg_ids); i++) {
+		reg_id = px30_saved_cru_reg_ids[i];
+		px30_saved_cru_regs[i] = readl_relaxed(px30_cru_base + reg_id);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void px30_clk_resume(void *data)
+{
+	int i, reg_id;
+
+	for (i = ARRAY_SIZE(px30_saved_cru_reg_ids) - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
+		reg_id = px30_saved_cru_reg_ids[i];
+		writel_relaxed(px30_saved_cru_regs[i] | 0xffff0000,
+			       px30_cru_base + reg_id);
+	}
+}
+
+static const struct syscore_ops px30_clk_syscore_ops = {
+	.suspend = px30_clk_suspend,
+	.resume = px30_clk_resume,
+};
+
+static struct syscore px30_clk_syscore = {
+	.ops = &px30_clk_syscore_ops,
+};
+
 static void __init px30_clk_init(struct device_node *np)
 {
 	struct rockchip_clk_provider *ctx;
@@ -1010,6 +1059,7 @@
 		pr_err("%s: could not map cru region\n", __func__);
 		return;
 	}
+	px30_cru_base = reg_base;
 
 	clk_nr_clks = rockchip_clk_find_max_clk_id(px30_clk_branches,
 						   ARRAY_SIZE(px30_clk_branches)) + 1;
@@ -1039,6 +1089,8 @@
 
 	rockchip_register_restart_notifier(ctx, PX30_GLB_SRST_FST, NULL);
 
+	register_syscore(&px30_clk_syscore);
+
 	rockchip_clk_of_add_provider(np, ctx);
 }
 CLK_OF_DECLARE(px30_cru, "rockchip,px30-cru", px30_clk_init);
